  "result": "The command now prints 27.06, and all 6 tests pass.\n\n**Root cause:** the price-parsing function `to_cents` in `shop/money.py:9` padded the decimal part with the zero on the wrong side. `\"5\".rjust(2, \"0\")` gives `\"05\"`, so `12.5` was read as $12.05 (1205 cents) instead of $12.50 (1250 cents). That made the quote 2 \u00d7 12.05 = 24.10, plus 8.25% tax = 26.09.\n\n**Fix:** I changed `rjust` to `ljust`, so `\"5\"` becomes `\"50\"`. Now 2 \u00d7 12.50 = 25.00, plus 2.06 tax = 27.06.\n\nI also checked `to_cents` on other inputs: `12.05`, `12`, `12.34` and `0.5` all give the right amounts (1205, 1200, 1234, 50).\n\n**Tests:** pytest isn't installed here, so I ran them with `python3 -m unittest`. None of the existing tests covers a price with one decimal place, which is how this bug got through. I didn't add one, but a test for `to_cents(\"12.5\") == 1250` would catch it if it came back.",
